WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
It's easy to complain that our maps of Mars&nbsp;
and Venus are way more detailed than our maps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.840
of our own Earth's ocean. But listen, Mars and&nbsp;
Venus don't have a bunch of water getting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.960
the way of mapping them. We can just throw&nbsp;
satellites up there and take pictures. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.360
map the ocean floor to a resolution of at least&nbsp;
100 meters, we need ships equipped with sonar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.980
which create maps by beaming out sound waves&nbsp;
at the ocean floor and measuring how long it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.060
takes for them to get back. We’d only need about&nbsp;
200 of these ships to map the whole damn ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.260
It would take like a year. Though, I'll admit,&nbsp;
running these ships for a year with a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:34.860
personnel on board ain't cheap. The good news&nbsp;
is that someone is working on this. Nippon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.540
Foundation and GEBCO got together to start&nbsp;
the Seabed 2030 with the goal of getting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:44.640
detailed map of our oceans by 2030. They're&nbsp;
also investing in faster and cheaper methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.100
like these little cute submarine robots&nbsp;
that take unmanned expeditions into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.900
ocean to create even higher resolution maps.&nbsp;
This is like a leap forward that will allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.820
future generations to explore more of Earth's&nbsp;
surface than all other generations combined.

